@flexiarg test-registry/warrant-rides-the-handoff
@title The Warrant Travels With The Handoff
@keywords handoff, bell, packet, entry-id, base-sha, agent-interaction, integration
@audience agent coordination (Agency bells, whistles, packets), review commissioners
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-16 (claude-7; the missing integration — the registry CLI exists but no handoff path carries or consumes a warrant: no caller of futon3c.test-registry outside its own namespace and tests)
@references [test-registry/register-the-run test-registry/bind-warrant-to-the-diff inbox-zero/measure-against-the-remote hygiene/route-to-who-can-act]
@see-also buffer-cleaner/receipt-then-gate
@why a registry nobody's handoff points to is a second place to look, and reviewers under time pressure will rerun instead. The README-inbox-zero stale-base incident (a packet reviewed against a base 355 commits old) shows the handoff contract must name what it hands over: the base sha, and here also the warrant.
@how every author-to-reviewer handoff (bell, whistle, packet, review request) carries the base sha and the registry entry id of each test run it relies on, one per namespace command; the reviewer's reply carries the review entry id. A handoff without a warrant is legal but typed :unwarranted, and the reviewer's lane is then full rerun of the declared namespaces — priced, not forbidden. Agency can render the warrant fields in the delivered turn so the reviewer sees them without searching.
@violation-signature a review request whose only test evidence is prose counts; a warrant id for a different base sha than the diff under review; a review reply with no review record; a registry used only in demonstrations.
! conclusion: Handoffs carry base sha and warrant ids, replies carry review ids, and an unwarranted handoff is typed and routed to full rerun rather than silently rerun or trusted.

  + context: Coordination between agents runs through Agency bells and packets whose text is free-form.

  + IF:
    An author hands work to a reviewer and the work includes test evidence.

  + HOWEVER:
    Free-form handoff text drops structured evidence; reviewers cannot find a warrant they are not told about; and a warrant for yesterday's base is worse than none if it is trusted.

  + THEN:
    Put base sha and warrant entry ids into the handoff's structured fields; type the handoff :warranted or :unwarranted; have the reviewer's reply return its review entry id.

  + BECAUSE:
    Integration is where the saving is realised: the registry saves nothing if the reviewer never meets the warrant.

  + evidence: 2026-09-14 round trip worked because the reviewer (zai-7) was handed the author entry id explicitly; every WM review on 2026-09-16 stated base shas but no warrant ids, and reran.
